(Sorry for the double inclusion of the forwarded text in my
previous e-mail).

The included news item was published right before today's disaster.
If it was not so tragic, it should be funny. But we can learn
something useful from this item:

1. Even(!) technology has its own limitations...
2. Shooting shells to computer's calculated addresses is not like
   sending flames in return to an e-mail message according to its
   "Reply-To:" field...
3. Do not try betas and/or demos that "never used before" on alive
   people.

P.S.:  Don't flame me. My personal political outlook does not have
       any connection to this e-mail, and anybody who knows my
       outlook can believe me.

> ..Update from Israel 
> 
> IDF Sophisticated Weaponry

> 
> The IDF is using a very impressive new radar system with their 
                     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
> 155mm cannons.  The Doher Cannon Systems is able to track 

> incoming rockets fired by Hizbullah and target the location of 

> the source precisely.  The cannons, with a 20-kilometer (12-mile 

> range), then return fire.  

> 
> The IDF Spokesman said, "The technological advances in tracking 
                               ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
> were never used before.  That makes this whole operation ... more 
       ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
> precise, not hurting civilians."  (SNS..4/16).
           ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
> Emergency Response and Research Institute- 6348 N Milwaukee Ave, 
> Suite 312, Chicago, Illinois  60646  USA
> Internet e-mail: enn@emergency.com - WWW Page: 
> http://www.emergency.com
> Clark Staten, Publisher - Steve Macko, Managing Editor

