>     One day, after a long and celibate courtship, Bill and
his wife were
>     finally married. Following a festive wedding reception, they retired
>     to their honeymoon suite, eagerly awaiting to consummate their
>     marriage. The blushing bride sat on a bedroom chair and watched her
>     Bill disrobe for the first time. As he sat on the edge of the bed and
>     removed his shoes and socks, his wife could not help but notice that
>     his toes were unbelievably grotesque. They were bent and twisted, and
>     easily the ugliest toes that she had ever seen in her life.
>
>     "My god Bill", she gasped, "what's wrong with your toes"?
>     "Oh, I forgot to tell you", he said, "I had Toelio when I was a kid"!
>
>     "Toelio? Did I hear right ", asked his bride? "Don't you mean Polio"?
>
>     "No, I mean Toelio", he insisted!  "It's a rare disease of the toes".
>
>     "I never heard of it", she said.
>
>     Bill then stood up and dropped his trousers to the floor. She
>     gasped once again as his legs came into view. His knees were
>     even uglier than his toes. The kneecaps were gnarled, twisted to the
>     side, and covered with bumps.
>
>     "Geez honey snookums", she said, "what the hell happened to your knees"?
>
>     "I forgot to tell you", he replied, "I had Kneesils when I was a kid".
>
>     "Kneesils"? Are you kidding", she asked. "Don't you mean Measles"?
>
>     "No, I mean Kneesils", he said. "It's a rare disease of the knees".
>
>     "Well I never heard of that one either", confessed the bride.
>
>     Next, came the "coup de grace" as Bill grabbed the waistband of
>     his boxer shorts and slowly lowered them to the floor. When his
>     incredulous bride gazed at his naked genitals, she cried out,  "Oh no!
>     Don't tell me! Don't tell me! Let me guess!  When you were a kid you
>     had Smallcox, right"?

