---------- Forwarded message ----------
Date: Tue, 13 Aug 1996 11:30:51 -0700 (PDT)
From: Phil Agre <pagre@weber.ucsd.edu>
To: rre@weber.ucsd.edu
Subject: phone scams

[We tolerate far too many scams in this country.  Recently, for example,
I was overcharged by a factor of five when I made a few long-distance
phone calls from a phone booth in the lobby of the One Kendall Square
office building near MIT.  The excessive charges were on the order
of $50.  I had assumed that this was standard rip-off number 137, the
long-distance company that exists solely to charge inflated rates to
the unwary.  When I reached the long-distance company's representative
on the phone, however, he insisted up, down, and sideways that most of
the surplus charges were going to the building management.  It blew my
mind -- here, if they're telling me the truth, is a building management
that preys on its own tenants' customers!  The long-distance company
instantaneously -- like within about three seconds, and with a totally
convincing weary sigh -- refunded about $30 of the excess, as if this
were a standard procedure.  All totally legal, I'm quite sure.  Anyway,
here is the latest version of this scam -- again, totally legal I'm sure.]

=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=
